1
00:00:01,070 --> 00:00:03,230
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:03,870
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:05,520
And my name is Katherine.

4
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:08,470
And today at EnglishPod, we have a lesson for you about small talk.

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:09,150
That's right.

6
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:18,600
We continue with our series about short casual conversations you may have with your friends, somebody you haven't seen in a long time, or maybe even a co-worker.

7
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:19,600
That's right.

8
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:23,910
So in today's dialogue, we're going to listen to two people who work in the same building.

9
00:00:24,430 --> 00:00:26,490
They're going to talk a little bit about travel, right Marco?

10
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:27,760
Yeah.

11
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:31,960
And a little bit about recommending a place to go on vacation, maybe Europe.

12
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:37,880
And so we're going to have some great phrases as to speaking of this topic very briefly with a friend.

13
00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:38,880
All right.

14
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:39,640
Let's listen to today's dialogue.

15
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:43,880
Hey, Michelle.

16
00:00:49,970 --> 00:00:50,590
Good to see you.

17
00:00:50,810 --> 00:00:51,390
Are you at lunch?

18
00:00:51,790 --> 00:00:52,590
Oh, hi, Jim.

19
00:00:53,190 --> 00:00:54,150
No, I just got back.

20
00:00:54,630 --> 00:00:56,150
I thought you were on vacation now.

21
00:00:56,410 --> 00:00:57,910
No, I wish I was.

22
00:00:58,230 --> 00:00:59,990
I just got back from Spain, actually.

23
00:01:00,290 --> 00:01:01,110
Oh, wonderful.

24
00:01:01,970 --> 00:01:04,570
Have you been there before or was it your first time?

25
00:01:04,810 --> 00:01:05,490
My first time.

26
00:01:05,830 --> 00:01:08,490
I've traveled around Europe a lot, but this was my first time to Spain.

27
00:01:08,850 --> 00:01:10,990
It was amazing and the weather was just beautiful.

28
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:13,310
No rain and just sun, sun, sun.

29
00:01:13,970 --> 00:01:15,690
I'm so jealous of you.

30
00:01:16,050 --> 00:01:17,910
I've never been anywhere in Europe.

31
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:21,490
I've always dreamed of traveling around and seeing the sights.

32
00:01:21,950 --> 00:01:23,190
Well, I really recommend Spain.

33
00:01:23,690 --> 00:01:24,670
You really should go.

34
00:01:25,590 --> 00:01:28,490
Anyway, it's been great to catch up, but I must be going.

35
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:29,490
This is my floor.

36
00:01:29,870 --> 00:01:30,950
Speak again soon, I hope.

37
00:01:31,470 --> 00:01:31,710
For sure.

38
00:01:32,130 --> 00:01:32,430
Take care.

39
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:34,480
All right.

40
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:35,480
We're back.

41
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:42,360
So, now, let's take a look at some of the language on Language Takeaway.

42
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:42,700
Language Takeaway.

43
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:44,360
All right.

44
00:01:44,790 --> 00:01:47,430
In Language Takeaway, we've got a couple of words and phrases for you.

45
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:50,560
The first one is, I just got back.

46
00:01:50,890 --> 00:01:52,670
So, to get back from somewhere, Marco.

47
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:54,480
That's right, Jim.

48
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:55,810
Just got back from Europe.

49
00:01:56,640 --> 00:02:03,970
And when you say, I just got back, is I recently or very recently returned from somewhere.

50
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:05,720
That's right.

51
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:07,750
To get back from somewhere means to return from that place.

52
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:12,030
So, in this dialogue, Jim just got back from Spain.

53
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:14,390
Michelle just got back from lunch.

54
00:02:14,950 --> 00:02:16,690
This means to return from somewhere.

55
00:02:16,910 --> 00:02:17,170
That's right.

56
00:02:17,310 --> 00:02:22,150
So, it can be a place, not only a country, but it can be, for example, the office.

57
00:02:22,410 --> 00:02:23,770
I just got back from work.

58
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:26,990
Or I just got back from football practice.

59
00:02:27,230 --> 00:02:28,350
That's why I'm very sweaty.

60
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:29,760
Right.

61
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:35,000
So, this obviously means that you are now not in the same place where you just got back from.

62
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:35,760
No, I'm from America.

63
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,560
I went to Spain on vacation and I just got back from Spain.

64
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:41,900
That means I'm in America again.

65
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:42,700
Yeah, exactly.

66
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:44,600
You're not still in Spain.

67
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:45,600
No.

68
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:46,600
All right.

69
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:47,800
So, that's for our first word.

70
00:02:47,970 --> 00:02:48,730
Now, let's go on.

71
00:02:48,730 --> 00:02:52,670
And Michelle said, oh, I'm so jealous of you.

72
00:02:52,850 --> 00:02:53,090
All right.

73
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:55,870
Jealous is an adjective and a very, very common one.

74
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:57,710
You can use it in many different circumstances.

75
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:00,510
It means to want what someone else has.

76
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:01,390
That's right.

77
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:05,470
So, in this case, Michelle is jealous that Jim went on vacation.

78
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:07,610
So, she wishes she could go on vacation.

79
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:08,410
That's right.

80
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:09,850
I'm so jealous of you.

81
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:16,210
Or if you see someone who has a new pair of shoes and you like the shoes because you don't have them, you can say, I'm so jealous.

82
00:03:16,710 --> 00:03:17,450
I love your shoes.

83
00:03:17,830 --> 00:03:18,170
That's right.

84
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:20,920
And if you're jealous, you say, I am jealous.

85
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:24,610
Or if you're jealous of a person, you say, I am jealous of you.

86
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:28,920
And so, they were ending their conversation and Jim said, you know what?

87
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:31,610
It's been great to catch up with you.

88
00:03:31,810 --> 00:03:32,230
All right.

89
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:34,530
This is a really important phrase, to catch up.

90
00:03:35,320 --> 00:03:36,390
It means two different things.

91
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:38,290
And we're going to talk about one meaning here today.

92
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:47,450
To catch up with someone, like to talk to them about what they've been doing recently, means to learn about their life because maybe you haven't talked in a long time.

93
00:03:47,770 --> 00:03:48,310
That's right.

94
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:58,350
So, maybe you haven't seen your friend in two years and you go out for dinner to catch up, to talk about what has happened in your life recently.

95
00:03:58,960 --> 00:03:59,960
That's right.

96
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:06,640
Or if you see a friend on the street and you know that you haven't seen him or her in a long time, you can make a date to catch up.

97
00:04:06,670 --> 00:04:08,790
So, hey Marco, I haven't seen you in months.

98
00:04:09,390 --> 00:04:10,930
Let's catch up sometime soon.

99
00:04:11,130 --> 00:04:11,490
That's right.

100
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:16,520
And that means to share information and to find out how you have been recently.

101
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:16,860
Exactly.

102
00:04:17,580 --> 00:04:18,060
All right.

103
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:22,040
Now, when he was getting out of the elevator, he said, this is my floor.

104
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:24,800
Now, we know floor, but my floor, what is this?

105
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:26,440
My floor, your floor, whose floor?

106
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:26,940
Right.

107
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:28,800
This doesn't make much sense, does it?

108
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:35,360
Well, actually floor here means that this is the floor that I work on or the floor that I live on.

109
00:04:35,360 --> 00:04:36,920
It means the place I'm going to.

110
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:37,920
Right.

111
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:41,740
So, we know floor, the ground beneath our feet in a building.

112
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:46,050
But in this case, we're talking about one of the levels of a building.

113
00:04:46,310 --> 00:04:47,590
So, maybe the sixth floor.

114
00:04:47,850 --> 00:04:48,230
That's right.

115
00:04:48,490 --> 00:04:54,010
So, if I'm in my apartment building in the elevator, I can say, oh, stop the elevator.

116
00:04:54,170 --> 00:04:54,930
This is my floor.

117
00:04:55,290 --> 00:04:57,290
I want to get off the elevator.

118
00:04:57,360 --> 00:04:58,360
Right.

119
00:04:58,360 --> 00:04:59,070
This is where I live.

120
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:00,360
All right.

121
00:05:00,360 --> 00:05:01,800
So, a lot of great stuff there.

122
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:05,240
Why don't we take a break and listen to the dialogue again?

123
00:05:05,240 --> 00:05:08,520
Hey, Michelle.

124
00:05:14,250 --> 00:05:14,870
Good to see you.

125
00:05:15,090 --> 00:05:15,670
Are you at lunch?

126
00:05:16,050 --> 00:05:16,870
Oh, hi, Jim.

127
00:05:17,410 --> 00:05:18,430
No, I just got back.

128
00:05:18,890 --> 00:05:20,430
I thought you were on vacation now.

129
00:05:20,710 --> 00:05:22,170
No, I wish I was.

130
00:05:22,530 --> 00:05:24,270
I just got back from Spain, actually.

131
00:05:24,570 --> 00:05:25,390
Oh, wonderful.

132
00:05:26,230 --> 00:05:28,850
Have you been there before or was it your first time?

133
00:05:29,090 --> 00:05:29,750
My first time.

134
00:05:30,050 --> 00:05:32,770
I've traveled around Europe a lot, but this is my first time to Spain.

135
00:05:33,150 --> 00:05:35,210
It was amazing and the weather was just beautiful.

136
00:05:35,730 --> 00:05:37,610
No rain and just sun, sun, sun.

137
00:05:38,250 --> 00:05:39,970
I'm so jealous of you.

138
00:05:40,330 --> 00:05:42,190
I've never been anywhere in Europe.

139
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:45,760
I've always dreamed of traveling around and seeing the sights.

140
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:47,480
Well, I really recommend Spain.

141
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:48,960
You really should go.

142
00:05:49,880 --> 00:05:52,780
Anyway, it's been great to catch up, but I must be going.

143
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:53,760
This is my floor.

144
00:05:54,180 --> 00:05:55,220
Speak again soon, I hope.

145
00:05:55,740 --> 00:05:56,000
For sure.

146
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:56,720
Take care.

147
00:05:57,720 --> 00:06:00,160
All right, we're back.

148
00:06:01,290 --> 00:06:04,450
So, now let's take a look at four key phrases on Fluency Builder.

149
00:06:06,180 --> 00:06:07,020
Fluency Builder.

150
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:13,080
So, there are some keys to these small talk conversations and they are definitely the phrases.

151
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:16,480
The first one we have here is, good to see you.

152
00:06:16,580 --> 00:06:18,800
So, Jim says, hey, Michelle, good to see you.

153
00:06:19,180 --> 00:06:19,440
That's right.

154
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:27,280
So, we've chosen this phrase because it's very casual and very common to use as a polite way of saying, hey, how are you?

155
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:28,520
Good to see you.

156
00:06:28,520 --> 00:06:32,300
I'm actually showing you that I'm happy that I am talking to you.

157
00:06:32,490 --> 00:06:34,770
So, this is a very flexible phrase.

158
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:39,410
You can say it at the beginning of a conversation when you just see someone, hey, Marco, good to see you.

159
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:42,690
Or at the end, you can say, it was very good to see you.

160
00:06:42,790 --> 00:06:44,430
I hope to see you again soon.

161
00:06:44,810 --> 00:06:45,330
That's right.

162
00:06:45,510 --> 00:06:52,850
So, maybe you can use it at the beginning if you just begin to talk to someone or if you're saying goodbye, you can say, well, you know what?

163
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:55,840
I have to go now, but it's been good to see you.

164
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:57,440
So, very flexible phrase.

165
00:06:57,880 --> 00:06:58,200
That's right.

166
00:06:58,880 --> 00:06:59,880
That's right.

167
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:03,880
Now, when Michelle thought that Jim was on vacation, Jim said, no, no, no.

168
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:05,600
I wish I was.

169
00:07:06,140 --> 00:07:06,660
All right.

170
00:07:06,820 --> 00:07:07,500
I wish I was.

171
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:09,640
I thought you were on vacation.

172
00:07:10,100 --> 00:07:13,320
So, she thought that he was still on vacation in Spain.

173
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:18,580
And he says, well, I'm not because, you know, here I am, but I wish I was.

174
00:07:18,780 --> 00:07:22,240
I wish I was in Spain right now because Spain is so nice.

175
00:07:22,640 --> 00:07:22,960
That's right.

176
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:26,700
So, it may seem a little bit strange, but it's actually completing the idea.

177
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:32,680
And basically, we're using the verb to be because that's the question.

178
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:33,760
Are you on vacation?

179
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:37,200
No, I wish I was on vacation.

180
00:07:37,560 --> 00:07:39,500
So, that means he's not, but he wants to be.

181
00:07:39,720 --> 00:07:40,280
That's right.

182
00:07:40,720 --> 00:07:46,380
Okay, so now, he actually had to get going and he used this exact phrase.

183
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:49,360
He said, I must be going.

184
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:50,200
I must be going.

185
00:07:50,520 --> 00:07:51,600
Now, remember this as a phrase.

186
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:53,540
This is a good way to end a conversation.

187
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:55,300
Maybe you have to go to work.

188
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:57,320
Maybe you have your kids at home.

189
00:07:57,460 --> 00:07:58,740
You have to take them somewhere.

190
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,360
So, you say, I'm really sorry, I must be going, but let's see each other again soon.

191
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:04,080
That's right.

192
00:08:04,280 --> 00:08:06,260
So, you are politely ending the conversation.

193
00:08:06,340 --> 00:08:09,080
You are politely saying, I can't talk to you anymore.

194
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:13,240
I must be going now, but it's been great to talk to you.

195
00:08:13,340 --> 00:08:13,540
That's right.

196
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:15,480
Very polite.

197
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:16,480
So, remember this one.

198
00:08:16,480 --> 00:08:17,480
It's better than, I'm going now.

199
00:08:17,480 --> 00:08:18,080
We don't say that.

200
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:19,480
Yeah, or I have to go.

201
00:08:19,480 --> 00:08:22,420
Well, you can say, I have to go, but it's nice saying, oh, you know what?

202
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:26,600
I really like talking to you, but I must be going now.

203
00:08:26,600 --> 00:08:29,400
And Jim also finished by saying, we'll speak again soon.

204
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:31,480
And Michelle said, for sure.

205
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:32,460
For sure.

206
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:35,700
Now, Americans particularly love to say this phrase.

207
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:39,960
For sure means yes, or I agree, or definitely.

208
00:08:40,560 --> 00:08:41,560
Right.

209
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:42,660
You can use it as an interjection.

210
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:47,060
So, if I say, hey, Catherine, am I going to see you later on today?

211
00:08:47,480 --> 00:08:48,060
Yeah, for sure.

212
00:08:48,460 --> 00:08:48,840
For sure.

213
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:50,440
There we go.

214
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:53,260
So, you're saying yes, absolutely, definitely, for sure.

215
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:55,640
So, Marco, my birthday is Friday night.

216
00:08:55,640 --> 00:08:57,200
Are you going to come to the party?

217
00:08:57,200 --> 00:08:57,460
For sure.

218
00:08:58,200 --> 00:08:59,200
I wouldn't miss it for the world.

219
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:00,200
All right.

220
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:02,760
So, that's all we have for today.

221
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:08,840
Why don't we review everything we've taken a look at now and listen to the dialogue one last time.

222
00:09:08,840 --> 00:09:12,000
Hey, Michelle.

223
00:09:17,860 --> 00:09:18,480
Good to see you.

224
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:19,300
Are you at lunch?

225
00:09:19,660 --> 00:09:20,500
Oh, hi, Jim.

226
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:22,040
No, I just got back.

227
00:09:22,520 --> 00:09:24,040
I thought you were on vacation now.

228
00:09:24,320 --> 00:09:25,800
No, I wish I was.

229
00:09:26,140 --> 00:09:27,880
I just got back from Spain, actually.

230
00:09:28,180 --> 00:09:29,000
Oh, wonderful.

231
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:32,460
Have you been there before or was it your first time?

232
00:09:32,700 --> 00:09:33,380
My first time.

233
00:09:33,660 --> 00:09:36,380
I've traveled around Europe a lot, but this was my first time to Spain.

234
00:09:36,740 --> 00:09:38,800
It was amazing and the weather was just beautiful.

235
00:09:39,340 --> 00:09:41,220
No rain and just sun, sun, sun.

236
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:43,580
I'm so jealous of you.

237
00:09:43,940 --> 00:09:45,820
I've never been anywhere in Europe.

238
00:09:46,330 --> 00:09:49,370
I've always dreamed of traveling around and seeing the sights.

239
00:09:49,830 --> 00:09:51,090
Well, I really recommend Spain.

240
00:09:51,610 --> 00:09:52,570
You really should go.

241
00:09:53,490 --> 00:09:56,390
Anyway, it's been great to catch up, but I must be going.

242
00:09:56,750 --> 00:09:57,370
This is my floor.

243
00:09:57,790 --> 00:09:58,830
Speak again soon, I hope.

244
00:09:59,210 --> 00:09:59,610
For sure.

245
00:10:00,030 --> 00:10:00,310
Take care.

246
00:10:01,320 --> 00:10:02,320
All right.

247
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:06,280
So, this was another part of our small talk series.

248
00:10:06,560 --> 00:10:13,060
As you can see, very short, but very useful language used to talk very briefly with someone.

249
00:10:13,260 --> 00:10:13,940
It always happens.

250
00:10:14,060 --> 00:10:18,280
You meet somebody at the elevator or you just want to talk very quickly.

251
00:10:18,820 --> 00:10:28,340
Yeah, but you don't want to talk about anything that's too personal like someone's politics or their religious beliefs or their children because maybe you don't know them very well.

252
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:36,390
So, these are really great topics to talk about like business trips or vacation or the weather.

253
00:10:37,490 --> 00:10:40,950
English speakers love to talk about these when they don't know someone that well.

254
00:10:41,400 --> 00:10:41,610
That's right.

255
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:43,400
That's right.

256
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:49,960
So, if you have any questions or any comments about this lesson or you want to suggest any other topics, you can find us at EnglishPod.com.

